.ds q \N'34'
.TH XCOMPMGR 1 2022-02-19 __xorgversion__
.SH NAME
xcompmgr \- sample X compositing manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BI "xcompmgr [\-d " display "] [\-r " radius "]"
.BI "[\-o " opacity "] [\-l " left-offset "]"
.BI "[\-t " top-offset "] [\-z " filter "] [\-acCfFnsS]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xcompmgr
is a sample compositing manager for X servers supporting the XFIXES, DAMAGE,
and COMPOSITE extensions.  It enables basic eye-candy effects.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d\  display
Specifies the display to manage.
.TP
.BI \-r\  radius
Specifies the blur radius for client-side shadows.
.TP
.BI \-o\  opacity
Specifies the opacity for client-side shadows.
.TP
.BI \-l\  left-offset
Specifies the left offset for client-side shadows.
.TP
.BI \-t\  top-offset
Specifies the top offset for client-side shadows.
.TP
.BI \-I\  fade-in-step
Specifies the opacity change between steps while fading in.
.TP
.BI \-O\  fade-out-step
Specifies the opacity change between steps while fading out.
.TP
.BI \-D\  fade-delta
Specifies the time (in milliseconds) between steps in a fade.
.TP
.B \-a
Automatic server-side compositing.  This instructs the server to use the
standard composition rules.  Useful for debugging.
.TP
.B \-c
Client-side compositing with soft shadows and translucency support.
.TP
.B \-f
When \-c is specified, enables a smooth fade effect for transient windows like
menus, and for all windows on hide and restore events.
.TP
.B \-n
Simple client-side compositing. This is the default mode.
.TP
.B \-s
Server-side compositing with hard-edged shadows.
.TP
.B \-C
When \-c is specified, attempts to avoid painting shadows on panels and docks.
.TP
.B \-F
When \-f is specified, also enables the fade effect when windows change their
opacity, as with transset(1).
.TP
.B \-S
Enables synchronous operation.  Useful for debugging.
.TP
.BI \-z\  filter
Specifies how the screen is magnified while it is zoomed, see
.BR ZOOM :
.B nearest
to show every pixel as a sharp block, or
.B linear
for a smooth picture.
The default is nearest.
.SH ZOOM
.B xcompmgr
can magnify the whole screen around the pointer.
The zoom level is set through the root window property
.BR _PICOM_ZOOM ,
of type CARDINAL and format 32, holding a single value: the zoom level in
16.16 fixed point, that is, multiplied by 65536.
A missing property, or a value of at most 65536, means no zoom, and levels
above 16 are treated as 16.
picom-zoom reads the same property, so the same bindings drive both.
Typically a key or mouse binding of the window manager sets it; from a shell:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xprop \-root \-f _PICOM_ZOOM 32c \-set _PICOM_ZOOM 131072
xprop \-root \-remove _PICOM_ZOOM
.fi
.RE
.PP
The zoom level changes smoothly, and the point under the pointer stays where
it is, so the zoomed view follows the pointer.
The cursor itself is not magnified.
By default every pixel of the magnified screen becomes a sharp block, which
shows exactly what is drawn; with
.B \-z linear
the magnified screen is smoothed instead.
The zoom needs client-side compositing, so it does nothing with \-a.
.SH BUGS
Probably.  Please report any you find to
https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/xorg/app/xcompmgr/.
